{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 420,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you rank the most habitable places in our\nsolar system Venus lands pretty low, with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4890,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surface temperatures hot enough to melt lead\nand sulphuric acid rain."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9080,
    "dDurationMs": 2680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And yet it may have just jumped to the front\nof the pack."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11760,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact we may have detected the signature\nof alien life - Venusian life -for the first time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23380,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our searches for life-beyond Earth have tended\nto focus on the Martian subsurface and the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27029,
    "dDurationMs": 5230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ocean moons Enceladus and Europa and even\nthe methane lakes of Titan."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32259,
    "dDurationMs": 4021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And yet the horrible hellhole that is the\natmosphere of Venus has just yielded perhaps"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36280,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the most exciting lead for extraterrestrial\nlife."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38920,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Arguably, it’s our only lead - the faint\nsignature of the chemical phosphine in Venus’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44460,
    "dDurationMs": 3099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atmosphere, which may have been produced by\nliving organisms."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47559,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I joked more that the weirdest end\nto this very weird year would be the discovery"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52059,
    "dDurationMs": 1221,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of alien life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53280,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s see if 2020 can make up for itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56820,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, before we get to the evidence, let’s talk about Venus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59769,
    "dDurationMs": 4731,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s the brightest thing in the sky besides\nthe sun and moon, hanging just above sunrise"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64500,
    "dDurationMs": 1019,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and sunset."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65519,
    "dDurationMs": 5001,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s bright because it’s close and it’s\nbig, at 90% Earth’s diameter, and its our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70520,
    "dDurationMs": 1699,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "closest planetary neighbor."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72219,
    "dDurationMs": 4381,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In many ways it’s Earth’s twin - it’s\neven inside that band around the Sun where"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76600,
    "dDurationMs": 3430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "liquid water is possible - the so-called habitable\nzone."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80030,
    "dDurationMs": 4129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But for the longest time Venus was thought\nto be among the LEAST habitable places in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84159,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the solar system."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85159,
    "dDurationMs": 3471,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "See, Venus has what we call a runaway greenhouse\neffect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88630,
    "dDurationMs": 4589,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Its atmosphere is 100 times the mass of Earth’s\nand mostly carbon dioxide."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93219,
    "dDurationMs": 3771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This traps so much heat that the surface of\nVenus reaches a temperature hot enough to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96990,
    "dDurationMs": 3809,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "melt lead, and with a pressure that would\ncrush most submarines."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100800,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That atmosphere also sports a permanent thick\nlayer of sulfuric acid clouds,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106820,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So yeah, when we realized just out just how\nawful Venus was, our telescopes and hopes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112700,
    "dDurationMs": 2790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for finding ET swiveled outwards."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115490,
    "dDurationMs": 4739,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To the outer solar system - Mars, Enceladus\nand Europa in particular, and ultimately to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120229,
    "dDurationMs": 1561,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planets around other stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121790,
    "dDurationMs": 3459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Venus wasn’t ready to give up our attention\nso easily."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125249,
    "dDurationMs": 6651,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our landers and orbiters - Venera, Pioneer, and Magellan - caught hints of unusual atmospheric chemistry."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131920,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Various sulphur-bearing molecules were out\nof the expected equilibrium quantities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136849,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But non-living, or abiotic processes like\nvolcanism could still explain these."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142569,
    "dDurationMs": 4301,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The other weird thing is that the clouds of\nVenus appear to absorb the Sun’s light in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146870,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a weird way - more short wavelength visible\nand UV light is sucked up than expected, leading"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152400,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the yellow colour of the clouds and dark\npatches that change over time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156590,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This could be due to dust churned up from\nthe surface - or it could be gigantic colonites"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161230,
    "dDurationMs": 2170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of microbial life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163400,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although the surface is completely hellish,\nat around 50km altitude both the temperature"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168560,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and pressure are close to those at Earth’s\nsurface, with the only problem being that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172780,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pesky sulphuric acid rain."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175060,
    "dDurationMs": 5149,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But compared to the death-zone of Venus’s\nsurface, it’s a paradise, and we talked"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180209,
    "dDurationMs": 4831,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about the possibility of human cloud-city\nsettlements in a previous episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185040,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 1967, Carl Sagan and Harold Morowitz were\namong the first to suggest that life might"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190220,
    "dDurationMs": 5349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exist permanently aloft in Venus’s relatively\nhabitable upper atmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195569,
    "dDurationMs": 5981,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These guys speculated about both microbes\nand larger creatures, alien gas bags perhaps"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201550,
    "dDurationMs": 2920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supported by in-built hydrogen balloons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204470,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But all these ideas were still pretty fringe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207690,
    "dDurationMs": 3659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The attention of ET-hunters remained focused\noutwards."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211349,
    "dDurationMs": 6670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One of the most promising avenues in the search\nfor life is to detect so-called atmospheric"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218019,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "biosignatures - chemicals in a planet’s\natmosphere that are very hard to explain without"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222969,
    "dDurationMs": 1081,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the presence of life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224050,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are lots of ways to do this - for example\nseeing the effect on a star’s light as it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228270,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "passes through its own planets atmospheres."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231390,
    "dDurationMs": 5019,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another possibility is to look for the absorption\nof the planet’s own light as it emerges"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236409,
    "dDurationMs": 2021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from deep within its atmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238430,
    "dDurationMs": 7350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This can be done at far infrared and submillimeter\nradio wavelengths where the star’s own glare"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245780,
    "dDurationMs": 1900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doesn’t kill the signal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247680,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One possible biosignature in this range is\nphosphine, which absorbs photons of around"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252840,
    "dDurationMs": 2120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1.1mm wavelength."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254960,
    "dDurationMs": 6140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Phosphine is produced in abundance by some\nmicrobes, but not so easily produced by non-biological"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261110,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "processes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262110,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A team of researchers led by Professor Jane\nGreaves, mostly out of the United Kingdom,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267190,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decided to explore phosphine as a possible\nbiosignature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271410,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They thought they’d take a look at Venus\nusing the James Clerk Maxwell telescope - more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275810,
    "dDurationMs": 5270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a control to help guide their studies of\nplanets beyond our solar system, but not really"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281080,
    "dDurationMs": 3731,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expecting to see anything so close to home."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284811,
    "dDurationMs": 4179,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But to their extreme surprise, they found\nphosphine."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288990,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The team followed up with ALMA - the Atacama\nLarge Millimeter/submillimeter Array - and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294010,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "confirmed that there had to be phosphine in\nthe upper atmosphere of Venus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298950,
    "dDurationMs": 2300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Vastly more than expected."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301250,
    "dDurationMs": 2630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So there are two big questions here."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303880,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Is the detection of phosphine real?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306200,
    "dDurationMs": 3610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if so, how likely is it that the phosphine\ncame from life?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309810,
    "dDurationMs": 5930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, the fact that both JMCT and ALMA detected\nthe feature makes it pretty convincing that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315740,
    "dDurationMs": 3860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s real, although there’s still a chance\nthat both observatories just happened to see"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319600,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a dip in the exact spot where phosphene produces\nan absorption line."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324120,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Followup observations will confirm or refute\nthis pretty quickly, but the detection is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328190,
    "dDurationMs": 2870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "looking pretty solid at this point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331060,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okay assuming it is real, does this mean we’ve\nfound life?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335180,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, let’s first discuss why the presence of\na simple molecule might get people excited"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339960,
    "dDurationMs": 1980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about the possibility of life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341940,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So phosphine is a tetrahedtron - pyramid or\nd-4 - shaped molecule with one phosphorus"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348500,
    "dDurationMs": 2550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and 3 hydrogen atoms."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351050,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's a common byproduct of living metabolisms,\nalthough its also highly toxic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356850,
    "dDurationMs": 3711,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now you don’t need biological processes\nto make the stuff."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360561,
    "dDurationMs": 4539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It can form anywhere that you might have free\nphosphorus and hydrogen atoms."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365100,
    "dDurationMs": 5041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means anywhere that the more stable phosphorus\nand hydrogen-containing molecules might be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370141,
    "dDurationMs": 1479,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "broken apart."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371620,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, it’s formed deep beneath the\nsurfaces of Jupiter and Saturn and carried"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375720,
    "dDurationMs": 1290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377010,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve spotted the characteristic absorption\nfeatures of phosphine in the two gas giants"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381920,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- but no one screamed life, because there’s\na clear mechanism for producing that phosphine"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388580,
    "dDurationMs": 2770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by abiotic processes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391350,
    "dDurationMs": 2430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So why the excitement with Venus?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393780,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, although phosphine can be produced in\na number of different ways, it rarely lasts"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398800,
    "dDurationMs": 2250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "long no matter how it’s made."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401050,
    "dDurationMs": 5870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The stuff is very quickly oxidized - particularly\nin the highly acidic atmosphere of Venus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406920,
    "dDurationMs": 6370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means something on Venus is making phosphine\nfaster than it can be destroyed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413290,
    "dDurationMs": 4650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On Venus there are a few ways to make this\nstuff - you’d definitely get some in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417940,
    "dDurationMs": 2910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extreme heat and density near the surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420850,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You might get some produced by lightning strikes\nin the cloud layers, or by cosmic rays hitting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425280,
    "dDurationMs": 1910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the upper atmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 427190,
    "dDurationMs": 6220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Most of the non-biological production is likely\nto happen at the surface, and would take years"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433410,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to diffuse to the upper atmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436110,
    "dDurationMs": 7180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However the scientists calculate that almost\nall of the phosphene would be destroyed in that time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443290,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The abiotic production rate of phosphine is\nexpected to be 10,000 to a million times too"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448750,
    "dDurationMs": 3690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "low to produce the amount of phosphine that\nwas observed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452440,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we’re left with two possibilities - either\nthere’s some unknown non-biological process"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457620,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that produced this phosphine, or Venus has\nlife."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461360,
    "dDurationMs": 7460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Honestly, the former still seems the more\nlikely because, well, it’s never aliens"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468820,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "until every other possibility is ruled out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471780,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this may be the most promising lead to\nextraterrestrial life we’ve ever had, so"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477480,
    "dDurationMs": 3380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "let’s talk about what that life might look\nlike."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480860,
    "dDurationMs": 3610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Almost certainly it would be floating microbes\nlike Sagan and co."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484470,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "talked about half a century ago."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487170,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the requirements for life in the clouds\nof Venus are pretty strict."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 491450,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The entire lifecycle would have to remain\nin the regions with survivable temperature"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496230,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and pressure, and the critters need to somehow\nsurvive the crazy acidity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501870,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sara Seager from MIT and collaborators proposed\na lifecycle that might just do the trick."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507870,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Firstly, they assert that these critters are\nmicrobes living in tiny droplets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512490,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Liquid environments are universally thought\nto be essential to life, and in the extreme"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517000,
    "dDurationMs": 5449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dryness of the Venusian atmosphere free-floating\nmicrobes would quickly dry out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522449,
    "dDurationMs": 5981,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But those droplets would be mostly hydrochloric\nacid - maybe 85% concentration, with the rest"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528430,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "water."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529430,
    "dDurationMs": 5589,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By comparison, the most extreme extremophiles\non Earth can survive in pools with something"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535019,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like 5% concentration of sulphuric acid - in\nthe Dallol pools in Ethiopia."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540089,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So these Venutian microbes would have to be\nvery different to anything found on Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547129,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These droplets can only exist in a narrow\nrange in the atmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551519,
    "dDurationMs": 4781,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Droplets are expected to form at a particular\nheight, but as they find each other and grow"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556300,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they begin to fall back down into the crushing\noven of the lower atmosphere, where they quickly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560800,
    "dDurationMs": 1209,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evaporate again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562009,
    "dDurationMs": 4921,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how does a droplet-dwelling organism survive\nthis?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566930,
    "dDurationMs": 1969,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Seager and co. have an idea."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568899,
    "dDurationMs": 7811,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What if the microbes enter a resilient spore\nstate as their droplets descend and dry out?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576710,
    "dDurationMs": 7490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth bacteria can transform into dormant\nstates in adverse conditions - bacterial spores."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 584200,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We previously talked about how these spores\ncan survive crazy conditions - including the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589410,
    "dDurationMs": 5650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vacuum of space and the extreme temperatures\nand pressures of meteor impact or atmospheric"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595060,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reentry."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596060,
    "dDurationMs": 7180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what if Venusian microbes passed through\nsuch a spore state in the lower atmosphere?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603240,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The life cycle would look like this: microbes\nare in a metabolic state in sulphuric acid"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609300,
    "dDurationMs": 1860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "droplets in the upper atmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611160,
    "dDurationMs": 2210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those droplets find each other and grow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613370,
    "dDurationMs": 3519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps microbes multiply in this phase."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616889,
    "dDurationMs": 3881,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As they fall and their droplets evaporate\nthey enter a spore state."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620770,
    "dDurationMs": 5509,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These spores are extremely tiny and light,\nand so they float in the haze below Venus’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626279,
    "dDurationMs": 1281,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cloud banks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 627560,
    "dDurationMs": 6469,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Updrafts then carry spores into the temperature\nrange once again where they act as nucleation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634029,
    "dDurationMs": 7011,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "centers for new droplets to form, at which\npoint they become metabolically active again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641040,
    "dDurationMs": 3140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And, like, churn out a bunch of phosphene\nI guess."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644180,
    "dDurationMs": 2339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, nice story."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646519,
    "dDurationMs": 3260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The main stretch here is the whole sulphuric\nacid thing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649779,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We just don’t know if life can exist in\nthose conditions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653319,
    "dDurationMs": 4781,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "An important point here is that Venus’s\nhellish conditions are relatively young only"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658100,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perhaps only around 700 million years old."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660740,
    "dDurationMs": 3659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And before that they likely had oceans of liquid\nwater."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664399,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Any lifeforms in its atmosphere today must\nhave evolved from presumably much more sensible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670149,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "critters that existed prior to the runaway\ngreenhouse effect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674529,
    "dDurationMs": 5401,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Could microbes really evolve to withstand\nthe transition from a watery to a sulphuric-acid-y"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679930,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "environment?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680930,
    "dDurationMs": 2190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I guess we’re about to find out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683120,
    "dDurationMs": 2120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And how exactly will we find that out?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685240,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, the first thing is to confirm the presence\nof phosphine with more observations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689480,
    "dDurationMs": 4099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The previous observations were relatively\nbrief, but there’s now motivation to point"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693579,
    "dDurationMs": 3281,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our telescopes for much much longer at Venus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696860,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We'll need to look for other phosphine absorption\nfeatures also, and entirely different biosignatures."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704420,
    "dDurationMs": 4890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But ultimately we’re going to want to go\nto Venus, to search for life signatures there"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709310,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or, even better, to bring samples back to\nEarth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712670,
    "dDurationMs": 4729,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "NASA is about to decide between two proposals\nfor the next mission to Venus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717399,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Neither mission has a direct life-detecting\ninstrument, but that might change given these"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721839,
    "dDurationMs": 1021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "developments."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722860,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However it may be time to refocus our solar\nsystem explorations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727580,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ultimately we want to bring back samples of\nthe Venusian atmosphere - to actually get"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731709,
    "dDurationMs": 3810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "microbes under a microscope, if they exist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735519,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The discovery of alien life in our nearest\nplanetary neighbor would totally change our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740209,
    "dDurationMs": 3581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "calculations about the frequency of life in\nthe universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743790,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We may be forced to conclude that life is\ncosmically abundant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749290,
    "dDurationMs": 8039,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2020 is the year of weird microbes - but this\nlatest one, if it’s real, may prove extremely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757329,
    "dDurationMs": 8331,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "good news - granting us a grander perspective\non humanity’s place in a life-filled space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765660,
    "dDurationMs": 1100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey everyone."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766760,
    "dDurationMs": 2440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A few things before we get to comments."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769200,
    "dDurationMs": 3759,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First up, if you're looking for some more great science content, you should check out"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772960,
    "dDurationMs": 5860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Overview, on PBS Digital Studios' science\nand nature channel Terra."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778820,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Overview is hosted by Joe Hanson from It's\nOkay to Be Smart and combines mesmerizing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783949,
    "dDurationMs": 5930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "drone footage with deep science storytelling\nto reveal all the things shaping our planet"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789879,
    "dDurationMs": 1411,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the 10,000 foot view."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791290,
    "dDurationMs": 6620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So head over to Terra, and make sure to (politely)\ntell them Space Time sent you!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797910,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Next up, we just wanted to shout out again\nto everyone who helps us out on Patreon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802690,
    "dDurationMs": 3339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As always, your contributions make a huge\ndifference."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806029,
    "dDurationMs": 4781,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But today an extra big shoutout goes to Scott\nGray who’s contributing at the Big Bang"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810810,
    "dDurationMs": 2459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "level - Scott, thank you so much."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813269,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve used your contributions so far to\nbake a nice apple pie and send to welcome"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818149,
    "dDurationMs": 4401,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our Venusian neighbors to the solar system\n- or at least into our awareness of their"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822550,
    "dDurationMs": 2019,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "existence in the solar system."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824569,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We look forward to neighborly relations with\nthe gentle Venusian gas-bag civilization."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828869,
    "dDurationMs": 5582,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And, ultimately, to ally with them in war\nagainst the cosmic string entities in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834451,
    "dDurationMs": 2269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "heart of the Sun that we discussed recently."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836720,
    "dDurationMs": 5979,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, Scott, I guess thanks for contributing\nto the future ascendency of humanity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842699,
    "dDurationMs": 6271,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okay, last week we talked about post-quantum\ncryptography - possible solutions to the impending"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848970,
    "dDurationMs": 6599,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cryptography-cracking powers of quantum computers\nthat don’t themselves require quantum technology."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 855569,
    "dDurationMs": 5131,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We were delighted to find several crypto-geniuses\nin our audience who called us out on a few"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860700,
    "dDurationMs": 1770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "errors."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 862470,
    "dDurationMs": 6339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First up, Mina86 pointed out that our use\nof mixing colors as an analogy for one-way"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868809,
    "dDurationMs": 7201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "functions in RSA protocol is more accurately\nanalogous to Diffie-Helman key exchange."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876010,
    "dDurationMs": 3230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I agree, and we should have been more careful\nhere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879240,
    "dDurationMs": 4611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our point was to illustrate one-way functions\nthemselves, but the way we said it could have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883851,
    "dDurationMs": 2199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "been interpreted as illustrating RSA."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 886050,
    "dDurationMs": 2450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So thank you for catching that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888500,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Several of you noticed that we skipped a power\nof 2 in one of our graphics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893840,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We jumped form 256 to 1024."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 896560,
    "dDurationMs": 5860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I think it comes from my habit of skipping\ngenerations of processor in my computer upgrades."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 902430,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I go for every second Moore’s law doubling,\nwhich leaves my machines basically paralyzed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908550,
    "dDurationMs": 1370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about half the time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909920,
    "dDurationMs": 4990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the last catch - a few of you noticed\nI used the expression “factor a prime number”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914910,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- which is dumb, because the factors of a\nprime number are just itself and 1."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919600,
    "dDurationMs": 2529,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which I guess you don’t need a quantum computer\nfor."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922129,
    "dDurationMs": 5171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What I meant was factoring the product of\ntwo primes INTO those primes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 927300,
    "dDurationMs": 2500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anyway, thanks for keeping us straight."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929800,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Finally, a few of you expressed your distaste\nfor lattice-based encryption protocols."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935300,
    "dDurationMs": 4861,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Really, there were doubts that any salad-based\nprotocols would be meaty enough to resist"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940161,
    "dDurationMs": 2139,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum decryption."
    } ]
  } ]
}
